  "dom_id": "step-q348324",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14098036/assets/77accc09333167b5d086558ab56120a240f31e03c9c575a22d55f4d76b6f7c45.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the velocity of an object moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis. At time $t=1,$ the particle is located at the position $x=2.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=6?$\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(1)=2$, and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=1$ to $t=6.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(6)=2+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$",
